The 4th-generation <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/apple-tv/">Apple TV</a> 4K with an A19 chip and <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/siri/">Siri</a> AI support is available starting on November 13, and we were able to spend some time with it ahead of launch.<br/>
<br/>
<div class="center-wrap"><iframe title="Apple TV 4K Impressions" src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/x-AXPtU8d_Y" width="560" height="315" frameborder="0" allowfullscreen="allowfullscreen"></iframe><em><a href="https://www.youtube.com/user/macrumors?sub_confirmation=1">Subscribe to the MacRumors YouTube channel</a> for more videos.</em></div><br/>
&zwnj;Siri&zwnj; AI is the headline feature, and while it's not perfect, it works better than the prior version of &zwnj;Siri&zwnj;. &zwnj;Siri&zwnj; was able to pull up multiple movie and TV show recommendations and answer questions about content.<br/>
<br/>
Apple says &zwnj;Siri&zwnj; AI is in beta, and there's more work to be done. &zwnj;Siri&zwnj; AI wasn't great at answering follow-up questions, and only did so part of the time.<br/>
<br/>
There are far-field microphones in the set-top box, so you can just say "Hey &zwnj;Siri&zwnj;" to activate &zwnj;Siri&zwnj; without having to use the remote. The &zwnj;Apple TV&zwnj; can recognize up to six people, so you can get personalized recommendations and context when talking to &zwnj;Siri&zwnj;.<br/>
<br/>
The person who sets up the &zwnj;Apple TV&zwnj; needs to have an iPhone or <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/ipad/">iPad</a> that supports &zwnj;Siri&zwnj; AI for &zwnj;Siri&zwnj; AI to work on the &zwnj;Apple TV&zwnj;, and personal context also requires each user to have their own &zwnj;Siri&zwnj; AI-compatible device. The 2nd- and 3rd-generation &zwnj;Apple TV&zwnj; 4K models will get &zwnj;Siri&zwnj; AI with the <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/11/12/siri-ai-tvos-27-2-1/">upcoming tvOS 27.2.1 update</a>.<br/>
<br/>
Apple didn't change the design of the &zwnj;Apple TV&zwnj; 4K or update the look of the &zwnj;Siri&zwnj; Remote, but the remote does have new capabilities. It has a speaker built in so you can have it play a sound when it's lost, and Precision Finding is available if you have an <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/iphone-air/">iPhone Air</a>, <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/iphone-duo/">iPhone Duo</a>, or <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/iphone-17/">iPhone 17</a> and later (excluding <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/iphone-17e/">iPhone 17e</a>).<br/>
<br/>
The tvOS interface feels faster with the A19 chip, and it should be better for games if you play <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/apple-arcade/">Apple Arcade</a> titles on your TV. You can also pair four <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/homepod/">HomePod</a> speakers to the &zwnj;Apple TV&zwnj; for surround sound, a feature that's unique to the latest model.<br/>
<br/>
